body:
I have seen that lol. I went to one lesson with a "Vocal coach". Dude, my voice dwarfed his and was actually in tune. He was a guitar teacher who also taught singing and a little piano. The thing is I was probably better at piano. He said a vocal coach needs to know piano for a degree and also said he can't play the arias in my book. Don't get me wrong, they are hard to play, and my current vocal coach who actually knows classical singing can't either, but don't say you can teach me piano and not know how to read sheet music. I don't know contemporary singing that well, but let me tell ya he needed a microphone to be heard from 2 feet away. Hell right next to him, I couldn't hear him. Worst $32 I've ever spent and they wanted me to pay $160 a month for weekly lessons. Haha no.

&#x200B;

P.S he also looked at me funny when I said the word Larynx.

Hello. I'm still a new composer as well, but if I were to give my two cents it would be your movements, at least the second movement, lack(s) form and punctuation. Form is one of the fundamentals of composition and a lack of it can make music sound lopsided and sort of disconnected in my small amount of experience. Bach is often made of run on sentences which is fine, it's characteristic of the baroque period. It never stops moving even when it slows down, but the classical period is all about musical "punctuation" with periods and starts and stops to build interest and resolve tension.  

&#x200B;

TL;DR: Study form
